 &nbsp; 
 
 
 5. ⚡ Hakeem&#x27;s dream majority 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 House Minority Leader Hakeem Jeffries (D-N.Y.) celebrates while watching election night results in Washington last night. Photo: Kenny Holston/The New York Times House Minority Leader Hakeem Jeffries (D-N.Y.) is set to preside over a commanding Democratic majority after his party swept districts President Trump carried by double digits, Axios' Andrew Solender writes. 

 Why it matters: Democrats' landslide win will make it much, much easier for the likely next speaker to secure the gavel without a sweat and ignore the demands of his progressive wing. It's a major partisan win for Democrats as a whole, but the party's centrist wing is also hailing the result as a much-needed strategic victory over the left.

 Rep. Greg Stanton (D-Ariz.), a leader of the center-left New Democrat Coalition, told Axios in an interview that Jeffries "now has the space to utilize his full talents as speaker." The size of Democrats' victory, Stanton said, "allows him to govern without worrying about being leveraged by anyone." 🔎 Zoom in: Progressives — particularly " The Bloc ," a group made up of hard-charging leftists — had hoped to use a small majority to extract concessions from Jeffries.

 Many of them have declined to commit to supporting Jeffries as speaker and signaled they would use their votes as leverage to force votes on progressive legislation such as Medicare-for-All, defunding ICE and cutting aid to Israel. But the left's strategy is "going to look different from what it would have been with a smaller margin," one incoming House progressive told Axios, noting that "leaders have more flexibility when they have big majorities." 👓 Between the lines: Stanton noted that most of the incoming Democrats who won in purple and red districts were endorsed by the center-left New Dems — one of Jeffries' most reliable power bases in the caucus.

