 6. 🤖 AI loses everywhere 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Voters showed deep distrust of the AI race, approving dozens of data center moratoriums and electing a raft of candidates who favor slowing a $10 trillion buildout, including in Texas — the epicenter of the boom, Axios' Maria Curi writes. 

 Why it matters: The results were a warning shot to some of the nation's wealthiest and most powerful executives, many of whom are already working on ways to build a more palatable version of the revolution they are dead-set on winning. 🔬 Zoom in: All told, nearly two-thirds of 2028 data center capacity could face delays, although most political analysts don't see Democrats permanently blocking construction.

 Democratic challenger Gina Hinojosa's surprise victory in the Texas governor's race fundamentally scrambles the math for the AI buildout. She campaigned on keeping a moratorium on data center construction in place until at least mid-2027. The state accounts for 41% of new U.S. capacity expected in 2028, according to the research firm SemiAnalysis. Via X Democrats also won gubernatorial races in Ohio, Georgia and Arizona — the three states expected to have the most new data center capacity after Texas. The winners in Ohio and Georgia favor restricting construction.

 Out of 41 data center measures on the ballot across 10 states, about two dozen proposals designed to restrict or block development appeared to have passed based on initial returns . 💰 The other side: In California, a measure that would've enacted a 5% wealth tax on individuals and trusts with more than $1 billion in assets appeared headed for defeat after facing heavy opposition from Silicon Valley.

